HST Office: the File panel is a part of the document's window; moving the window keeps it open.

The File panel ("Tệp tin", also the Print page that Ctrl+P opens) was a weld::Popover: on Windows, macOS and gen a
floating popup window of its own. A press on the window's title bar closes every popup (vcl win salframe:
WM_NCLBUTTONDOWN calls ImplCallClosePopupsHdl), so dragging the window by its title bar closed the panel, and the
Print page cancelled its print job; with the mouse captured by the popup (after a click inside it) the press only
closed the popup and the window did not move at all. Win+arrow, maximize and restore left the popup where it was.
The reference suite keeps its File view while the window moves (user report: the print screen cannot be dragged
as in Word).

- sfx2/source/backstage/hstbackstagewindow.*: the panel is a child window of the document window's client window
  (BackstagePanelHost, an InterimItemWindow filled by the "panel" box of hstbackstage.ui), placed where the
  drop-down opened (BackstageWindow::GetPlacement, unchanged: under the ribbon and the document tab strip, left of
  the banner) and above the document and the ribbon's other children. It moves with the window; a resize of the
  window (maximize, restore, snap) places it again. It still closes on Esc, Alt+F, the File tab, the "←" of the
  title band and when one of its commands runs, and on a mouse press in its window outside the panel and the title
  band (an application event listener while it is open), so the document click still closes it. The keyboard
  handling, the pages and the Print page's job are unchanged.
- sfx2/source/backstage/hstbackstage.cxx: a click on the File tab that closed the open panel (a click outside it)
  does not open it again.
- sfx2/uiconfig/ui/hstbackstage.ui: the GtkPopover "HstFilePanel" around the "panel" box is gone.

License: these upstream files remain under their original license (MPL-2.0).
Test: tests/runtime/check-backstage.py (the panel is in the document's window and stays open when it moves).
